This toolkit is the open, Apache-2.0 reference layer extracted from the OMEGA
project. It is self-contained and does NOT include OMEGA's proprietary sector
engines, which remain under a separate licence and are not distributed here.

Dependency: `cryptography` (Apache-2.0 OR BSD-3-Clause), used for Ed25519, ES256
and ML-DSA-65 signatures.
Optional runtime tool: `openssl`, used for RFC 3161 timestamping.

Third-party material included in this repository:

- omega_evidence/pqbackends/vectors/nist_acvp_mldsa65.jsonl: ML-DSA-65 test cases from
  the NIST Automated Cryptographic Validation Protocol server
  (https://github.com/usnistgov/ACVP-Server, commit
  975de31eb83d87039ec88934fdc47d8c312b892d, files ML-DSA-sigVer-FIPS204 and
  ML-DSA-sigGen-FIPS204 internalProjection.json; SHA-256 in the file's first line).
  Source: the National Institute of Standards and Technology. Software developed by NIST
  employees is not subject to copyright protection within the United States; the NIST
  notice asks that modified works say what was changed: on 17 November 2026
  tools/build_nist_acvp_mldsa65.py (this repository) selected the cases by its own rule
  (every case of the ML-DSA-65 / external / pure sigVer group; the sigGen cases of the
  same groups with an empty context), dropped the secret keys and the hedged randomness,
  and wrote one JSON object per case. Up to 0.11.4 this repository carried instead
  vectors/acvp_mldsa65_sigver.txt, whose 10 sigVer lines and header were byte for byte
  those of crates/elara-record/tests/vectors/acvp_mldsa65_sigver.txt in
  navigatorbuilds/elara-mesh (AGPL-3.0); that file is removed in 0.12.0 and no line of
  it is in the new file.
- tests: test vectors and examples copied from IETF RFCs (Copyright (c) IETF Trust and
  the persons identified as the document authors; BCP 78 and the IETF Trust's Legal
  Provisions): RFC 8032 §7.1 (Ed25519), RFC 7517 Appendix A.1 (an EC public key),
  RFC 9964 Appendix A (an ML-DSA-65 public key and its kid), RFC 8785 Appendix B
  (number serialisation).

Not third-party: tests/fixtures/rfc6962_cryptovalid_vectors.json is computed with
cryptovalid-opencore, and omega_evidence/_tsa_keyid.py is ported from
cryptovalid-opencore's cryptovalid_tsa.py; both are by the same author, who licenses
these files here under Apache-2.0.
